    "https://careers.ua.edu/jobs/law-school-reference-and-research-librarian-530746-tuscaloosa-alabama-united-states": {
      "title": "Law School Reference and Research Librarian",
      "description": "Law School Reference and Research Librarian - 530746 - Tuscaloosa, Alabama, United States My settings Featured Jobs Research Healthcare Information Technology (IT) Life at UA Campus Life Tuscaloosa Benefits Work Life Search Jobs Job Alerts Staff Jobs Student Jobs Faculty Jobs UA System Jobs My Application Kiosk mode Law School Reference and Research Librarian - 530746 Tuscaloosa, Alabama, United States Apply Now Search by job title, location, department, category, etc. Search Law School Reference and Research Librarian - 530746 Tuscaloosa, Alabama, United States Library/Research/Scientific Closing at: Nov 5 2026 - 22:55 CST Pay Grade/Pay Range: Minimum: $57,200 - Midpoint: $74,300 (Salaried E9) Department/Organization: 218161 - Law Library Normal Work Schedule: Monday - Friday 8:00am to 5:00pm Note to Applicants: Position is eligible for hybrid work subject to University policy . Job Summary: The Law School Reference and Research Librarian assists in providing an enhanced level of legal research assistance to the Law School's faculty as well as to other members of the Law School community. Additional Department Summary: The Reference and Research Librarian is responsible for providing research and support services to the law school and broader university communities. This includes providing exceptional reference services and assistance to students, faculty, staff, and library visitors; teaching or co-teaching legal research sessions and/or guest lectures within the law school; serving as a liaison for the law school’s student-run law journals, moot court programs, and legal clinics; and engaging heavily with outreach initiatives related to library services and collections. The Reference and Research Librarian is responsible for functional supervision of students performing reference services. In addition, they participate in collection development activities and engage in professional activities based on interest, skill, and library needs. Required Minimum Qualifications: Juris doctorate, master's degree in library science, and some library research, reference, and service experience. Skills and Knowledge: Ability to work in a team-based setting where consultation, flexibility, collaboration, cooperation, and resilience. Effective verbal and written communication skills. Ability to work both independently and collaboratively. Demonstrated enthusiasm for working closely with student populations. Ability to manage projects and balance competing priorities. Demonstrated project management skills and/or formal training in project management or workflow optimization/evaluation. Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree in library and information science (or equivalent degree) from an ALA-accredited library or information science school. Juris Doctor from an ABA-accredited law school. Background Investigation Statement: Prior to hiring, the final candidate(s) must successfully pass a pre-employment background investigation and information obtained from social media and other internet sources. A prior conviction reported as a result of the background investigation DOES NOT automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration for this position. A candidate with a prior conviction or negative behavioral red flags will receive an individualized review of the prior conviction or negative behavioral red flags before a hiring decision is made. Equal Employment Opportunity : The University of Alabama is an Equal Employment/Equal Educational Opportunity Institution. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment or volunteer status without regard to any legally protected basis and will not be discriminated against because of their protected status. Applicants and employees of this institution are protected under Federal law from discrimination on several bases. More information is available in the EEOC’s Know Your Rights: Workplace discrimination is illegal poster. The University of Alabama affirm